WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
<v Narrator>Curiosity is at the center of cultural humility, but not every question is curious

2
00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:07.350
in a useful way.

3
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.900
Compare two questions from the readings.

4
00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:15.650
One asks, “Why are people from your community reluctant?” The other asks, “What part

5
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:21.000
is difficult to use?” The first assigns a group explanation before the person

6
00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:22.350
speaks.

7
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.700
The second points at the actual task and invites the person's own account.

8
00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:31.850
Professional curiosity is strongest when it has a clear purpose.

9
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.200
A question about someone's whole cultural history may be interesting to you and

10
00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:39.750
still be unnecessary for the task.

11
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.700
Before you ask, try a quick test.

12
00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:45.650
What will I do with the answer?

13
00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:51.800
If you cannot say, the question may be serving your interest more than the person's

14
00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:53.150
need.

15
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.300
<v Narrator>Curiosity can also become a burden.

16
00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:02.050
In the readings, the staff member wants to understand the person he is serving

17
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:06.600
better, so he asks about the family tradition behind seeking advice.

18
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:12.350
She answers, “I would rather just compare the travel arrangements.” That is not a

19
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.700
refusal of help or a sign of distrust.

20
00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:19.450
It tells him which question she wants answered now.

21
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:25.200
So he goes back to the travel times and explains what the appointment process

22
00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:26.550
allows.

23
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:32.700
If she later chooses to share more, he can listen, without assigning her account to

24
00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:34.850
everyone with a similar background.

25
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.400
Practical help must never depend on someone sharing a personal story.

26
00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:44.350
And no one should be treated as the spokesperson for a culture.

27
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:48.900
Hearing one person's account can improve your understanding of the moment.

28
00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:53.050
It does not tell you what a whole group wants.

29
00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:55.400
<v Narrator>What about background reading?

30
00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.150
Community briefs can help.

31
00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:01.700
They introduce history, language and experiences with institutions that you might

32
00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:03.050
otherwise miss.

33
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.800
The risk is using a brief as the answer.

34
00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:12.550
One reading names the trap directly: a claim about “how Hmong families are” turns

35
00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.700
background information into a stereotype.

36
00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:20.050
Reading that some communities value family involvement in planning does not tell you

37
00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:25.800
whom this person wants involved, what they wish to share, or whether the meeting

38
00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:27.550
arrangements work for them.

39
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:30.500
So turn the background into open questions.

40
00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:35.050
Then listen for what fits, what differs and what is missing.

41
00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:38.000
Watch the premise in your wording too.

42
00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:44.550
In the bowl example, the researcher is tempted to ask the bowl's owner why she is

43
00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.700
not following the proper custom.

44
00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:50.450
That question already treats a pattern as a rule.

45
00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:55.400
Asking what matters to her about everyday use leaves her answer open.

46
00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:59.350
<v Narrator>Hook and his colleagues add a useful idea.

47
00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.900
They call it cultural opportunities: moments in a conversation when someone's

48
00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.850
identity or background comes to the front.

49
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:12.600
Noticing those moments, and choosing to explore them with care, is a skill people

50
00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:13.950
can learn.

51
00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.300
The same authors note that anxiety reduces curiosity.

52
00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:22.650
So part of the work is building comfort with these conversations, through practice

53
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.800
and reflection rather than lectures.

54
00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:28.950
They also stress that no single identity explains a person.

55
00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:34.300
Someone is never only their language, their age, their faith or their disability.

56
00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:40.450
When an opening appears, ask one relevant question, and make it easy to decline: the

57
00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:42.600
person chooses what to share.

58
00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:46.950
<v Narrator>Listening and sharing influence are not the same thing.

59
00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.500
Go back to the two friends.

60
00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:54.850
The first friend owns the vehicle for the move and proposes leaving early.

61
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:59.400
The second friend says the timing makes an important goodbye hard.

62
00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:03.150
The first friend says, “I understand,” and changes nothing.

63
00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:04.500
Listening happened.

64
00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:05.850
Influence did not.

65
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:12.000
The first friend may have a real limit: the vehicle must be returned that afternoon.

66
00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:15.350
Humility does not mean promising an impossible schedule.

67
00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:20.700
It means separating the real limit from a preferred departure time, then exploring

68
00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:22.450
what is actually possible.

69
00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:24.600
Work has the same pattern.

70
00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:29.150
A supervisor announces an assignment, then asks the employee for ideas.

71
00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.500
The employee cannot tell what is still open.

72
00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:38.650
A clearer approach says what is decided, what remains open, and how a concern could

73
00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:40.400
affect the next step.

74
00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:45.350
Honest limits protect trust better than an invitation that cannot change anything.

75
00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:49.300
<v Narrator>Finally, notice how your role shapes the encounter.

76
00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:55.450
Staff often choose the questions, the order of an explanation, the words in a record

77
00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:58.000
and the route a request takes.

78
00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:01.350
That is influence, even in an ordinary exchange.

79
00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:07.500
In the readings, the staff member writes a short summary for the staff who handle

80
00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:08.850
appointments.

81
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:15.400
His first draft says, “The person has not made a decision.” That is accurate as far

82
00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:19.550
as it goes, but it leaves out her actual question.

83
00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:23.300
The next reader may simply send reminders to choose.

84
00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:28.650
His revision says she wants to compare travel conditions with someone she trusts

85
00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:32.800
before choosing, and that the allowed arrangements still need checking.

86
00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:38.950
Cultural humility asks you to look at that kind of influence, not give up the

87
00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.700
responsibility attached to it.

88
00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:46.450
The way you carry someone's question forward can keep their meaning or quietly lose

89
00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:47.800
it.

